course: dictionary
lesson: 12594
title: Discern: seeing what is hard to see
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To discern means to see, hear or notice something that is hard to see or hear. It can also mean to understand something, often a difference, by thinking carefully.
explain: «In the thick fog, we could barely discern the outline of the barn.» The fog makes the barn hard to see, so discern means to make out, or to see with effort. Try the check: swap in notice and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «Sam could discern a pattern in the numbers.» Sam did not just look at the numbers. He thought carefully and noticed how they were connected. That is the second meaning of discern.
explain: In a formal sense, discern means to tell apart, such as right from wrong. «It can be hard to discern right from wrong when you are angry.» Here the word is about thinking clearly, not about seeing.
fact: Discern is a verb. Its forms are discern, discerns, discerned and discerning.
fact: To discern means to see, hear or notice something that is hard to see or hear.
fact: Discern can also mean to understand something, often a difference, by thinking carefully.
fact: Useful phrases: «hard to discern», «discern between», «discern a pattern».
fact: In a formal sense, discern means to tell apart, such as right from wrong.
exercise: «In the dark, we could barely discern the outline of the barn.» What does discern mean here? | To forget where you are | To run away quickly | *To see or make out something that is hard to see | To sing loudly
exercise: «Sam could discern a pattern in the numbers.» What does discern mean? | *To notice or understand something by thinking carefully | To count every number one by one | To lose track of time | To paint in bright colours
exercise: «He discerned the sound of a bell far away.» Which word is the past tense of discern in this sentence? | discern | discerning | discerns | *discerned
test: «It is hard to discern right from wrong when you are angry.» What does discern mean in this formal sense? | To forget the rules | To shout at someone | To give up | *To tell apart
test: «The old man could discern his friend's face through the mist.» What does discern mean? | To hide from someone | To jump over a wall | *To see something that is hard to see | To buy a present
exam: Which word could replace discern in «Can you discern the difference between the two coins?» | Throw | Forget | Hide | *Tell apart
